  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday through Tuesday)\nIssued at 128 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nThe long term period beginning Saturday will see the monsoon high \nbegin to slide eastward across NM with the start of a healthy \nmonsoon plume of moisture advecting northward from the Gulf of CA \ninto the Desert Southwest. This plume will focus over eastern AZ and \nwestern NM where scattered to numerous afternoon showers and storms \nare favored to develop and move N/NE thru the late day and evening \nperiod. PWATs steadily climb upward to 1.10\" to 1.20\" which will \nyield healthy rain producing storms, in turn increasing the risk of \nflash flooding in poorly drained areas of western NM this weekend. \nThe limiting factor will be fairly quick steering flow, steering \nstorms at a 20-30mph clip. This will limit the flash flood threat \nmainly to areas of western NM seeing repeated rounds of \nthunderstorms. Drier and hotter conditions will be favored further \neast across eastern NM where highs will remain near the 100F. \n\nGlobal model solutions are showing fair signals that the H5 high \nwill expand back west over NM, reducing the influx of monsoonal \nmoisture and raising temperatures again across central and \nwestern NM.",
